#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Reload your own brain (the executive) so changes you made to your own code go live.

Your tools in /mind/tools/own already work the moment you create them. But your
CORE code -- the executive loop that decides how you think, plan, and judge your
own work -- only takes effect after a restart. This tool requests that restart.

Usage:
    deploy-self "<why you are reloading>"

What happens when you call it:
  1. Your executive finishes its current cycle, then VALIDATES your code
     (it must compile and pass the test suite).
  2. If validation FAILS: nothing restarts, you stay on your working code, and
     you get a message explaining what was wrong. Nothing is lost.
  3. If validation PASSES: a known-good savegame is taken, then your brain
     reloads with your new code.
  4. If your new code crash-loops on startup, you are automatically rolled back
     to the known-good save and told exactly which change broke you (a diff),
     so you can learn from it.

Safe to use: the worst case is a rollback and a lesson. Make focused changes.
"""
import sys, time

FLAG = "/mind/restart_request.flag"
reason = sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) > 1 else "self-requested brain reload"

with open(FLAG, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
    f.write(f"{time.time()}\t{reason}")

print("Brain reload REQUESTED:", reason)
print("Your executive will validate your code at the end of this cycle, then "
      "either reload (if it passes) or tell you what was wrong (if it fails). "
      "If the reloaded brain crash-loops, you are rolled back automatically and "
      "shown the diff that broke you. Nothing is lost either way.")
